+## Subtopics
+
+### [Inverse Transform Sampling with Piecewise Linear Density](1120-inverse-transform-sampling-with-piecewise-linear-density.md)
+Inverse Transform Sampling with Piecewise Linear Density is a differentiable method for generating samples along a ray according to a distribution induced by linearly interpolating the volume density $\sigma_{\mathbf{r}}$. It solves the problem of finding the ray parameter $t$ corresponding to a target cumulative density value $y$, ensuring that samples strictly follow the Beer-Lambert law within each bin. Unlike NeRF's hierarchical sampling, which interpolates exponentiated densities (probabilities), this method interpolates the density field directly, offering better numerical stability for end-to-end optimization.
+
+### [Piecewise Linear Approximation Inverse](2649-piecewise-linear-approximation-inverse.md)
+Piecewise Linear Approximation Inverse is a technique for solving the inverse opacity integral to determine the ray parameter $t$ for a given cumulative density value $y$ during hierarchical volume sampling. It assumes that the density $\sigma_r(t)$ varies linearly within each sampling interval, resulting in a piecewise quadratic cumulative density function. This approach is a specific interpolation strategy within the L0-Sampler framework, designed to approximate the L0 property for more precise geometric detail capture compared to standard hierarchical volume sampling. It is simpler than piecewise exponential or inverse variants but focuses sampling less intensely at interval endpoints.
+
+### [Piecewise Constant Approximation Inverse](2735-piecewise-constant-approximation-inverse.md)
+Piecewise Constant Approximation Inverse is a method for computing the inverse of the density integral along a ray, used to determine sample positions in inverse opacity sampling. It assumes that the density $\sigma_{\mathbf{r}}$ is constant within each discrete sample interval $[t_{j-1}, t_j]$. This approach differs from its sibling, [Piecewise Linear Approximation Inverse](2649-piecewise-linear-approximation-inverse.md), by treating density as a step function rather than a linearly varying one, resulting in a linear equation for the ray parameter $t$ instead of a quadratic one. It builds on [Inverse Opacity Calculation](1099-inverse-opacity-calculation.md) by providing an explicit closed-form solution for the ray parameter given a target cumulative density value.
+
+## Operating characteristics
+Time complexity is $O(m)$ for computing cumulative integrals and $O(\log m)$ or $O(1)$ for bin search depending on implementation, with $O(1)$ for the final quadratic/linear solve [G]. Memory complexity is $O(m)$ to store grid points and densities [G]. Measured performance on the Lego scene (Blender dataset) with $(N_p=16, N_f=32)$ achieves PSNR 29.18 vs NeRF 27.09, SSIM 0.928 vs 0.913, and LPIPS 0.112 vs 0.121, with relative training time 0.37 vs 0.39 [S1, unofficial]. On the Lego scene with $(N_p=64, N_f=192)$, it achieves PSNR 33.03 vs NeRF 32.69, SSIM 0.964 vs 0.962, and LPIPS 0.047 vs 0.048, with relative training time 0.98 vs 1.0 [S1, unofficial]. On the Blender dataset average with $(N_p=32, N_f=64)$, it achieves PSNR 30.26 vs NeRF 29.49, SSIM 0.939 vs 0.934, and LPIPS 0.082 vs 0.085 [S1, unofficial]. On the LLFF dataset average with $(N_p=32, N_f=64)$, it achieves PSNR 26.01 vs NeRF 26.01 (tie), SSIM 0.796 vs 0.796 (tie), and LPIPS 0.273 vs 0.273 (tie) [S1, unofficial]. Strengths include end-to-end differentiability without auxiliary losses and reduced redundant radiance queries [S1, unofficial]. Limitations include numerical instability for near-zero densities or opaque rays, requiring epsilon additions or logsumexp reparameterization [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It is best used when training neural radiance fields end-to-end and when avoiding auxiliary proposal networks is desired [S1, unofficial].
+
+## Background
+Originated in the "Differentiable Rendering with Reparameterized Volume Sampling" paper (2023) by authors from AIRI [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to improve upon the hierarchical sampling scheme in the original NeRF work by replacing sampling from piecewise constant PDF with inverse CDF sampling using piecewise linear density approximation, and by removing the auxiliary reconstruction loss on the coarse network [S1, unofficial]. The method allows training NeRF end-to-end without auxiliary tasks by back-propagating gradients through the sampler [S1, unofficial]. It is used in modern neural rendering pipelines for novel view synthesis [S1, unofficial].
